  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1130 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nFriday through Tuesday:\n\nSynopsis: Mainly seasonable temperatures will prevail through the \nlong term period, with occasionally muggy conditions mixing in. \nMultiple chances for showers and thunderstorms will exist \nalong/ahead of a pair of passing upper disturbances & affiliated \nsurface lows & fronts. Currently favored passage windows for peak \nforcing & precip potential are Friday through Saturday night & \nSunday through Tuesday. Not expecting showers & storms to be ongoing \nfor the entirety of either time frame, with intermittent windows of \ndry conditions mixing in with the periods of showers and storms. \nWill continue to refine timing details over coming updates. An \nunseasonably moist environment will accompany storm development \nduring the Friday-Saturday night time frame, with locally heavy \nrainfall possible in locations experiencing multiple batches of \nshower/storm activity. Where any such locally heavy rainfall may \noccur, and how widespread its occurrence may be, remains uncertain \nthis far out, with trends being monitored through the second half of \nthis week.  \n\nFriday through Saturday night: Showers & storms will likely be \nongoing near or just to the west of the region Friday morning \nfollowing the arrival of an upper disturbance & attendant surface \ncyclogenesis in the Northern Plains Thursday night. With the low \ncontinuing into the Upper Mississippi Valley on Friday, suspect that \nat least scattered shower and storm potential will try to spread \neast into southern Wisconsin during the morning & afternoon hours \nFriday. Global ensembles point toward very marginal instability \nthrough Friday afternoon, implying minimal strong/severe potential \nin any of this activity. Brief heavy downpours would be possible \ngiven steadily increasing precipitable water values. If current \ntiming trends hold, more favorable ingredients for possible locally \nheavy rainfall would begin to align overnight Friday into Saturday \nmorning, when a deep pull of warm/moist advection would bring even \nhigher precipitable water values into the area as the low level jet \nconcurrently triggers additional storm development. Whether \ningredients will come together to realize locally heavy rainfall \nwill depend on storm-scale details (namely training), which will \nclarify with the arrival of high-res guidance later this week. Could \nthen see additional storm potential along a passing cold front \nSaturday afternoon/evening, with favored locations for redevelopment \nbeing dependent on still unclear boundary positioning. Will be \nmonitoring position trends over coming updates.  \n\nSunday through Tuesday: Second disturbance will move across the area \nfrom the Northern Plains, interacting with the weekend frontal \nboundary to support additional chances for showers and a few storms. \nLarge spread in frontal location & upper disturbance track make it \ndifficult to offer additional details regarding timing or the \npotential for additional heavy downpours at this time, with details \nexpected to clarify as this portion of the period draws closer.\n\nQuigley",
